<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Passion of Saints Perpetua and Felicity</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">203</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Passion of Saints Perpetua and Felicity is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> account of the martyrdom of a group of Christians in the amphitheatre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> in 203. It includes a prison diary attributed to Vibia Perpetua, a young noblewoman, making it one of the earliest surviving texts written by a Christian woman. Her slave Felicity, who gave birth in prison, died with her.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/passion-of-saints-perpetua-and-felicity.jpg" alt="Passion of Saints Perpetua and Felicity" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benedictine abbey of Saint Gallen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Passio_sanctarum_Perpetuae_et_Felicitatis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
